  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<para>Here are graphs showing how the amount of money in each of the purses change. Remember, Purse A starts with $1,000 and grows by $200 each day. Purse B starts with $0.01 and doubles each day.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of an exponential function, origin O. number of days and amount in dollar."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/728c20d8311a5f32ef4aba9fc24217f0d41c30cf"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
<para>Which data on the graph shows the amount of money in Purse \(A\)? Which data on the graph shows the amount of money in Purse \(B\)? Explain how you know.</para>
</problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The blue data on the graph containing \(P\) is Purse \(B\), and the red data on the graph containing \(Q\) is Purse \(A\). Purse \(B\) starts with less money than Purse \(A\), so its graph will be lower at the beginning.The blue data on the graph containing \(P\) is Purse \(B\), and the red data on the graph containing \(Q\) is Purse \(A\). Purse \(B\) starts with less money than Purse \(A\), so its graph will be lower at the beginning. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Points \(P\) and \(Q\) are labeled on the graph. Explain what they mean in terms of the genie’s offer. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Point \(P\) says that 9 days after the genie has appeared, Purse B contains $5.12. Point \(Q\) says that 5 days after the genie has appeared, Purse A contains $2,000. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What are the coordinates of the vertical intercept for each graph? Explain how you know. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> For Purse A, the vertical intercept is \((0,1,000)\) because when the genie appears (day 0), the purse has $1,000. For Purse B, the vertical intercept is \((0,0.01)\) because it only contains $0.01 when the genie appears. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> When does Purse B become a better choice than Purse A? Be prepared to show your reasoning. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> According to the graphs, on day 19 and afterward, Purse B is worth more than Purse A. This can be seen on the graph since Purse B has a greater \(y\)-coordinate for the first time when \(x\) is 19. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Knowing what you know now, which purse would you choose? Be prepared to show your reasoning. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample: 

      Purse \(B\) is worth more after 19 days, but there may be other considerations to take into account.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>While on a beach, your friend discovers a different genie. This genie also offers two purses to choose from, and he gives you the following graph to show how the money in each purse will grow. The set of triangular marks that lie on a line represent values in Purse A, and the set of square marks that make a curve represent those in Purse B.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of 2 functions on grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/af054ac5d2c2686ff0434298ac81349b314fe1e4"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>The genie is still deciding how many days he will let the money in the purses grow. Help your friend plan a strategy for picking the purse with more money while the genie thinks.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Your answers may vary, but here is an example:</para>
    <para>The vertical intercept for Purse B is lower, so Purse B starts off with less money than Purse A. The values in Purse B stay lower than the values in Purse A until day 10, when they appear to be the same. So Purse A is better up to day 10, and after that point Purse B becomes a better choice.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


